{"name":"New Sled","hex":"#933c3c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"new","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-3 language code for Newar."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Recently made, or created.","Of recent origin; having taken place recently."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/new"},{"term":"sled","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, light vehicle with runners, used recreationally, mostly by children, for sliding down snow-covered hills; no draft animal pulls it.","A vehicle on runners, used for conveying loads over the snow or ice, and often pulled by sled dogs."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To ride a sled.","To convey on a sled."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sled"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sled","blocks":[{"text":"A sled, sledge, or sleigh is a vehicle that slides across a surface, usually of ice or snow. It is built with a smooth underside or a separate body supported by runners that reduce friction with the surface. Some designs are pulled by humans, animals, or machines to transport passengers or cargo across relatively level ground. Others are designed to travel downhill for recreation or competition. Terminology varies by region, for example \"sled\", \"sledge\", and \"sleigh\" have different common uses in British, American, and Australian English."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Volunteer_Mushing_on_Wonder_Lake_%287065286379%29.jpg/330px-Volunteer_Mushing_on_Wonder_Lake_%287065286379%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sled","wikidata":"Q181388"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Portrait_of_Dwight_and_Inet_Holcombe_sledding%2C_Stillwater%2C_Minnesota_-_DPLA_-_3462d0d5451f8ff27ed9332d27385fbf_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Portrait_of_Dwight_and_Inet_Holcombe_sledding%2C_Stillwater%2C_Minne